    "q-106251": {
      "dom_id": "step-q106251",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find a point that satisfies the mean value theorem for $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ where the position vectors of $A$ and $B$ are $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩,$ respectively.",
      "worked_solution": "The mean value theorem states that if $f(x,y,z)$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB},$ then there exists a point $C\\in \\overset{―}{AB}$ with position vector $c$ such that\n\n$$\nf(b)-f(a)=∇f(c)·(b-a).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ is differentiable at each point on the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, we can apply the mean value theorem.\n\nSubstituting $a=⟨0,2,1⟩$ and $b=⟨1,0,-1⟩$ into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,-1)-f(0,2,1) & =∇f(c)\\cdot (⟨1,0,-1⟩-⟨0,2,1⟩) \\\\ 2-(-7) & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $∇f$ and evaluating at $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f & =⟨2x,-4y,2z⟩ \\\\ ∇f(c) & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =∇f(c)\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =⟨2{c}_{1},-4{c}_{2},2{c}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-2,-2⟩ \\\\ 9 & =2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $C$ lies on $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ So, let's first find the equation of the line.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-0}{1} & =\\frac{y-2}{(-2)}=\\frac{z-1}{(-2)} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{y-2}{2}=-\\frac{z-1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nParametrizing the line, we get\n\n$$\nx=t,\\,\\,y=2-2t,\\,z=1-2t,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSince $c=⟨{c}_{1},{c}_{2},{c}_{3}⟩$ satisfies the equation of the line, we have\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=t,\\,\\,{c}_{2}=2-2t,\\,{c}_{3}=1-2t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above in the equation $2{c}_{1}+8{c}_{2}-4{c}_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(t)+8(2-2t)-4(1-2t) & =9 \\\\ 2t+16-16t-4+8t & =9 \\\\ -6t & =-3 \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now find the values of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2-2(\\frac{1}{2})=1, \\\\ {c}_{3} & =1-2(\\frac{1}{2})=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the coordinates of $C$ are $(\\frac{1}{2},1,0).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
